Before extracting anything, restate the target data model in your own words, in five or six sentences:

- a **track** is one sequential line of work; steps in the same track never overlap, and anything that happens while something else happens belongs in a different track;
- a **step** has exactly one duration kind: `fixed` (`seconds`), `variable` (`minSeconds`, `maxSeconds`, `defaultSeconds`) or `indefinite` (runs until the person ends it);
- a **startTrigger** ties a step to the clock or to another step — `programStart`, `programStartOffset`, `afterStep`, `afterStepWithBuffer`, `manual`, `onAbort`, `compound`;
- `stepId`s are unique across the WHOLE program, not per track;
- every `task` a step occupies needs a matching `resourceConstraints` entry, and that entry's `maxConcurrent` is what stops two steps sharing one oven, one thermocycler or one PA;
- work repeated n times (n trays, n samples, n aircraft) is `replicates: {count: n, mode: "serial" | "parallel" | "stagger"}` on ONE step — never n hand-copied steps or tracks. Chain what follows each repetition with `{"type":"afterStep","stepId":"<step>","instances":"each"}`, give the step that waits for all of them `{"instances":"all"}`, and when the holding area between them only fits k at a time (a rack that holds two trays, a rotor that holds six tubes, a taxiway that holds two aircraft) set `replicates.maxInFlight: k` so the upstream step is held back instead of stranding the downstream one.

`rhylthyme://guide/authoring` is the full cheat-sheet, and `rhylthyme://schema/program` the schema, if you want to check a rule.

Then list the resource constraints you expect this workout to need — one `task` per line with the `maxConcurrent` you intend to declare and how many people are working — and end with that list as JSON in a single ```json fenced block:

```json
{
  "acknowledgement": "tracks are sequential lines of work; one duration kind per step; triggers link steps; every task needs a resourceConstraint; stepIds are global",
  "resourceConstraints": [
    { "task": "oven", "maxConcurrent": 1 },
    { "task": "burner", "maxConcurrent": 2 },
    { "task": "prep", "maxConcurrent": 2 }
  ],
  "actors": 2
}
```

## Tracks

Five tracks capture the parallel lines of work: a **group-floor** track for the two moments all three athletes are doing the same thing together (warm-up, cooldown); one track each for **athlete-a**, **athlete-b**, **athlete-c**, holding that athlete's own sequential chain of station→rest→station→…→station (no rest after round 4); and a small **sled-equipment** track that holds the one physical-reset step the sled needs between users, since that reset belongs to the equipment, not to any one athlete, and would otherwise force two different athletes' steps into the same track.

## Triggers

`warm-up-floor` starts at `programStart`. Every athlete's round-1 station chains `afterStep` off `warm-up-floor`. Within each athlete's track, every station and every rest chains `afterStep` off the immediately preceding step in that same track — this keeps all three tracks in lockstep automatically, since every station and every rest is 60 s, except where the sled forces a gap (below). The two sled hand-offs are where a plain `afterStep` isn't enough: `round3-b-sled` needs both B's own rest to be over *and* the sled to be reset, so it uses a `compound` "all" of `afterStep rest-b-round2` and `afterStep sled-reset`; `round4-a-sled` needs both A's rest to be over *and* a reset gap after B's sled turn, expressed as a `compound` "all" of `afterStep rest-a-round3` and `afterStepWithBuffer round3-b-sled` (20 s). `cooldown-floor` is a `compound` "all" of `afterStep` on each athlete's round-4 station, so it only starts once every athlete is actually done.

## Refining the dependency question

A plain "which activities depend on which, and which can run at the same time?" only asks about the *work* (stations, rests, warm-up, cooldown) and misses what the coach's note is actually warning about. The better question for this text is: **"Which shared piece of equipment needs to be physically reset or repositioned between users, and does that reset ever take longer than the rest period the next user is already taking — meaning the equipment, not the athlete, becomes the thing everyone is waiting on?"** Answering it: the sled is pushed "the length of the turf and back" and sits "at the far end of the turf," so after 60 s of work it isn't sitting ready at the start line the way a kettlebell or box is — someone (or something) has to get it back into position, and the coach explicitly calls this the "longest reset," strongly implying it exceeds the ordinary 60 s window baked into everyone else's rest. That reset is never named as a step in the text, but the rotation cannot work without it, so it's added here as one inferred step, `sled-reset` (task `sled`, fixed 90 s — longer than the 60 s floor rest, which is the whole point), sitting between `round2-c-sled` and `round3-b-sled`. Its effect is folded into the triggers above: `round3-b-sled` waits on it directly, and `round4-a-sled` waits on an equivalent buffer after `round3-b-sled`, so the hand-off risk the coach flagged is actually represented in the schedule instead of assumed away.

